1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,285
Picture your evening.

2
00:00:01,305 --> 00:00:02,397
The couch. The blanket.

3
00:00:02,417 --> 00:00:06,998
The phone. Many of us now spend around
four hours a day on our phones.

4
00:00:07,018 --> 00:00:08,473
Scrolling. Watching.

5
00:00:08,493 --> 00:00:12,791
Swiping. Videos, games,
messages, music, all in one hand.

6
00:00:12,811 --> 00:00:14,356
And still, we say it.

7
00:00:14,376 --> 00:00:17,275
I'm bored. Now take the phone away.

8
00:00:17,295 --> 00:00:19,800
No phone. No TV. No internet.

9
00:00:19,820 --> 00:00:22,111
For three hundred thousand years.

10
00:00:22,131 --> 00:00:26,748
Now your brain is probably saying, they
must have been bored out of their minds.

11
00:00:26,768 --> 00:00:29,536
Sitting around. Staring at the fire.

12
00:00:29,556 --> 00:00:31,412
Waiting for something to happen.

13
00:00:31,432 --> 00:00:33,391
At least, that's what we assume.

14
00:00:33,411 --> 00:00:38,641
But archaeologists keep digging up game
boards, toys, flutes, and even a joke

15
00:00:38,661 --> 00:00:41,354
that's nearly four thousand years old.

16
00:00:41,374 --> 00:00:44,633
So what did our ancestors
actually do for fun?

17
00:00:44,653 --> 00:00:47,395
To find out, start with
something surprising.

18
00:00:47,415 --> 00:00:49,660
Fun is older than humans.

19
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:54,879
In the nineteen nineties, a scientist
named Jaak Panksepp started tickling young

20
00:00:54,899 --> 00:00:59,942
rats. The rats chirped back, at a
pitch far too high for our ears.

21
00:00:59,962 --> 00:01:02,393
He suspected it was a kind of laughter.

22
00:01:02,413 --> 00:01:07,113
And when he stopped, the rats came
running back to his hand, asking for more.

23
00:01:07,133 --> 00:01:12,097
Apes laugh too. Tickle a young chimp, and
it pants, ha, ha, ha, a breathless kind

24
00:01:12,117 --> 00:01:16,795
of giggle. In two thousand nine,
researchers recorded tickled orangutans,

25
00:01:16,815 --> 00:01:20,620
gorillas, chimps, bonobos,
and human babies.

26
00:01:20,640 --> 00:01:23,654
The sounds lined up like a family tree.

27
00:01:23,674 --> 00:01:29,042
Laughter seems to go back to an ancestor
we all share, roughly ten to sixteen

28
00:01:29,062 --> 00:01:30,645
million years ago.

29
00:01:30,665 --> 00:01:32,608
Young chimps even play pretend.

30
00:01:32,628 --> 00:01:38,063
In Kibale, Uganda, young female chimps
carry sticks around like dolls.

31
00:01:38,083 --> 00:01:42,301
They carry them, rest with them,
and sometimes even take them to bed.

32
00:01:42,321 --> 00:01:46,289
And they seem to stop once they
have real babies of their own.

33
00:01:46,309 --> 00:01:51,202
Young animals of many kinds chase,
wrestle, and play fight, and nobody has to

34
00:01:51,222 --> 00:01:54,447
teach them. So fun didn't start with us.

35
00:01:54,467 --> 00:01:56,017
We inherited it.

36
00:01:56,037 --> 00:01:59,029
But did ancient humans
even have time for fun?

37
00:01:59,049 --> 00:02:03,405
Surely they were busy, every hour
of the day, just staying alive.

38
00:02:03,425 --> 00:02:08,322
In the Philippines, a team of scientists
tracked the Agta, people who still hunt,

39
00:02:08,342 --> 00:02:14,060
fish, and gather. Agta who mostly foraged
had around ten more hours of free time

40
00:02:14,080 --> 00:02:16,835
every week than Agta who mostly farmed.

41
00:02:16,855 --> 00:02:19,587
Ten extra hours. Every week.

42
00:02:19,607 --> 00:02:23,049
Farming brought more food,
but it also brought more work.

43
00:02:23,069 --> 00:02:27,461
Back in the nineteen sixties, Richard
Lee found that Ju'hoansi adults in the

44
00:02:27,481 --> 00:02:32,049
Kalahari spent only about two and
a half days a week getting food.

45
00:02:32,069 --> 00:02:36,737
Add in cracking nuts, cooking, and making
tools, and it came closer to a forty

46
00:02:36,757 --> 00:02:39,524
hour week. Not endless free time.

47
00:02:39,544 --> 00:02:42,097
But not dawn to dusk toil either.

48
00:02:42,117 --> 00:02:46,497
Now you might be thinking, these are
people living today, not ancient humans.

49
00:02:46,517 --> 00:02:50,504
And you're right. But they're the best
clue we have to how foragers spent their

50
00:02:50,524 --> 00:02:54,279
days. So what did people
do with their free time?

51
00:02:54,299 --> 00:02:55,467
First, they talked.

52
00:02:55,487 --> 00:03:00,462
In the Kalahari, the anthropologist Polly
Wiessner recorded the conversations of

53
00:03:00,482 --> 00:03:02,487
Ju'hoansi hunter-gatherers.

54
00:03:02,507 --> 00:03:06,650
During the day, people talked
about work, food, and complaints.

55
00:03:06,670 --> 00:03:09,250
Stories were less than one in ten.

56
00:03:09,270 --> 00:03:12,830
But at night, around the fire, more
than eight out of ten long group

57
00:03:12,850 --> 00:03:14,880
conversations were stories.

58
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:17,442
Funny stories. Scary stories.

59
00:03:17,462 --> 00:03:19,705
Stories about people far away.

60
00:03:19,725 --> 00:03:22,480
Stories about who shared,
and who was greedy.

61
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:24,767
Who was brave, and who ran away.

62
00:03:24,787 --> 00:03:27,699
The rules of the camp,
hidden inside the fun.

63
00:03:27,719 --> 00:03:30,036
And being a great storyteller paid off.

64
00:03:30,056 --> 00:03:35,244
In two thousand seventeen, the researcher
Daniel Smith asked Agta people who they

65
00:03:35,264 --> 00:03:37,181
would most like to live with.

66
00:03:37,201 --> 00:03:41,869
Skilled storytellers were picked nearly
twice as often, even ahead of good

67
00:03:41,889 --> 00:03:43,494
hunters and fishers.

68
00:03:43,514 --> 00:03:47,089
And on average, they had
more children who survived.

69
00:03:47,109 --> 00:03:52,202
Many of their stories were about cooperation,
sharing, and men and women being

70
00:03:52,222 --> 00:03:55,014
equal. The fire was their TV.

71
00:03:55,034 --> 00:03:57,641
And the storytellers were the stars.

72
00:03:57,661 --> 00:03:58,852
Then there were games.

73
00:03:58,872 --> 00:04:04,139
Among the Hadza of Tanzania, men play
a game of chance called lukuchuko.

74
00:04:04,159 --> 00:04:08,527
They toss discs of bark at a
tree, and bet things like arrows.

75
00:04:08,547 --> 00:04:13,089
Winners are expected to keep playing,
so prized things keep moving around the

76
00:04:13,109 --> 00:04:15,623
camp, and nobody ends up hoarding them.

77
00:04:15,643 --> 00:04:17,623
Other games used bones.

78
00:04:17,643 --> 00:04:21,161
Knucklebones are the ankle
bones of sheep and goats.

79
00:04:21,181 --> 00:04:24,559
Each side lands differently,
so they work like dice.

80
00:04:24,579 --> 00:04:29,609
In Israel, archaeologists found more than
six hundred of them in one spot, about

81
00:04:29,629 --> 00:04:32,084
two thousand three hundred years old.

82
00:04:32,104 --> 00:04:35,222
Some were marked with
game words, like, stop!

83
00:04:35,242 --> 00:04:39,359
And in places like Mongolia,
people still play with them today.

84
00:04:39,379 --> 00:04:44,527
In southeastern Turkey, archaeologists
opened a grave about five thousand years

85
00:04:44,547 --> 00:04:49,164
old. Inside were forty
nine small carved stones.

86
00:04:49,184 --> 00:04:51,652
Pigs. Dogs. Little pyramids.

87
00:04:51,672 --> 00:04:55,229
They might be some of the
oldest game pieces ever found.

88
00:04:55,249 --> 00:05:00,036
In Egypt, people were playing a game
called Senet more than four thousand five

89
00:05:00,056 --> 00:05:01,424
hundred years ago.

90
00:05:01,444 --> 00:05:05,961
Over time, it became linked to the
journey of the soul after death.

91
00:05:05,981 --> 00:05:10,849
Tutankhamun was buried with at least four
game boxes, so he could keep playing in

92
00:05:10,869 --> 00:05:14,884
the afterlife. And then
there's the Royal Game of Ur.

93
00:05:14,904 --> 00:05:19,797
It's about four thousand five hundred
years old, found in royal graves in what is

94
00:05:19,817 --> 00:05:24,257
now Iraq. For a long time,
nobody knew the rules.

95
00:05:24,277 --> 00:05:28,770
Then Irving Finkel, at the British
Museum, studied a clay tablet written by a

96
00:05:28,790 --> 00:05:30,520
Babylonian scribe.

97
00:05:30,540 --> 00:05:32,132
On it were the rules.

98
00:05:32,152 --> 00:05:34,045
Now people can play it again.

99
00:05:34,065 --> 00:05:39,382
Two players race their pieces across
the board, rolling pyramid-shaped dice.

100
00:05:39,402 --> 00:05:42,195
Land on a rosette, and you roll again.

101
00:05:42,215 --> 00:05:45,810
People kept playing it for
well over two thousand years.

102
00:05:45,830 --> 00:05:50,372
A version of it was still played in
southern India in the nineteen fifties.

103
00:05:50,392 --> 00:05:55,085
Someone even scratched its board into
the stone of a giant palace statue.

104
00:05:55,105 --> 00:05:58,222
Possibly a guard. A bored guard.

105
00:05:58,242 --> 00:06:03,309
So be honest. For one evening in the
past, would you play the Royal Game of Ur,

106
00:06:03,329 --> 00:06:06,380
or knucklebones? Tell me in the comments.

107
00:06:06,400 --> 00:06:07,816
Kids had toys, too.

108
00:06:07,836 --> 00:06:12,620
In the Indus Valley, more than four thousand
years ago, children may have played

109
00:06:12,640 --> 00:06:15,970
with little clay carts
and animals on wheels.

110
00:06:15,990 --> 00:06:19,808
Some animals even had heads
that bobbed on a string.

111
00:06:19,828 --> 00:06:23,471
Hunter-gatherer kids today
still make their own toys.

112
00:06:23,491 --> 00:06:28,211
Little bows, tiny digging sticks,
and dolls, copying the grown-ups.

113
00:06:28,231 --> 00:06:33,411
Much older, in a cave in France,
archaeologists found a small bone disc with a

114
00:06:33,431 --> 00:06:34,724
hole in the middle.

115
00:06:34,744 --> 00:06:37,299
An animal is carved on each side.

116
00:06:37,319 --> 00:06:40,642
Standing on one. Lying down on the other.

117
00:06:40,662 --> 00:06:44,742
Some researchers think it hung on a
string, and when you spun it, the animal

118
00:06:44,762 --> 00:06:48,017
seemed to move. It could
also have been a tool.

119
00:06:48,037 --> 00:06:53,154
But if it was a toy, it might be one
of the oldest animations ever made.

120
00:06:53,174 --> 00:06:57,137
And all over the world, people
played with a simple loop of string.

121
00:06:57,157 --> 00:07:02,962
Hands, fingers, a few twists, and suddenly,
there's a house, a star, or a fishing

122
00:07:02,982 --> 00:07:08,274
net. One explorer in the nineteen tens
recorded well over a hundred Inuit string

123
00:07:08,294 --> 00:07:09,453
figures.

124
00:07:09,473 --> 00:07:10,880
Some fun was louder.

125
00:07:10,900 --> 00:07:15,230
Deep in Egypt's desert, there's a cave
covered in painted people, and many of

126
00:07:15,250 --> 00:07:17,093
them look like they're swimming.

127
00:07:17,113 --> 00:07:19,130
Swimming. In a desert.

128
00:07:19,150 --> 00:07:23,330
They were painted around seven thousand
years ago, when the Sahara was greener,

129
00:07:23,350 --> 00:07:28,420
with lakes. Though experts still argue
about whether they're really swimming.

130
00:07:28,440 --> 00:07:33,683
In Egypt, about four thousand years ago,
a tomb wall was painted with wrestlers.

131
00:07:33,703 --> 00:07:38,897
Not two or three. About two hundred
pairs, each one in a different hold.

132
00:07:38,917 --> 00:07:42,167
It's like a four thousand
year old wrestling manual.

133
00:07:42,187 --> 00:07:45,103
In the same tomb, women
are painted juggling.

134
00:07:45,123 --> 00:07:48,130
It's the oldest known picture of juggling.

135
00:07:48,150 --> 00:07:53,385
In Mexico, archaeologists found the oldest
rubber balls we know of, about three

136
00:07:53,405 --> 00:07:55,598
thousand six hundred years old.

137
00:07:55,618 --> 00:07:58,323
And they had a recipe for extra bounce.

138
00:07:58,343 --> 00:08:02,460
Tree latex, mixed with juice
from a morning glory vine.

139
00:08:02,480 --> 00:08:05,779
The balls were made for a
game played with the hips.

140
00:08:05,799 --> 00:08:10,967
And in Alaska, Iñupiat communities
celebrate a good whaling season by tossing

141
00:08:10,987 --> 00:08:16,429
each other high into the air, from
a blanket of seal or walrus skins.

142
00:08:16,449 --> 00:08:17,867
It's still done today.

143
00:08:17,887 --> 00:08:21,685
Along with contests like
the one foot high kick.

144
00:08:21,705 --> 00:08:23,585
And of course, there was music.

145
00:08:23,605 --> 00:08:28,697
In caves in Germany, archaeologists found
flutes made from bird bone and mammoth

146
00:08:28,717 --> 00:08:31,585
ivory, about forty thousand years old.

147
00:08:31,605 --> 00:08:35,697
One, made from a vulture's wing
bone, has five finger holes.

148
00:08:35,717 --> 00:08:38,237
Copies of it can still play a tune.

149
00:08:38,257 --> 00:08:43,679
In some Ice Age caves, paintings tend
to sit where sound echoes the most.

150
00:08:43,699 --> 00:08:47,895
Some researchers think that was on
purpose, though it's still debated.

151
00:08:47,915 --> 00:08:50,370
Where there's music, there's dancing.

152
00:08:50,390 --> 00:08:54,532
Some of the oldest pictures of people
dancing come from the first farming

153
00:08:54,552 --> 00:08:58,498
villages of the Middle East,
about nine thousand years ago.

154
00:08:58,518 --> 00:09:02,661
And in the forests of Central
Africa, the Aka sing in layers.

155
00:09:02,681 --> 00:09:05,981
Every voice a different
tune, weaving together.

156
00:09:06,001 --> 00:09:10,943
The United Nations now lists it as
part of the world's cultural heritage.

157
00:09:10,963 --> 00:09:12,349
But here's my favourite.

158
00:09:12,369 --> 00:09:14,436
Ancient people told jokes.

159
00:09:14,456 --> 00:09:19,407
The oldest joke we know of comes from
Sumer, in what is now Iraq, nearly four

160
00:09:19,427 --> 00:09:20,882
thousand years ago.

161
00:09:20,902 --> 00:09:22,382
It goes like this.

162
00:09:22,402 --> 00:09:25,820
Something which has never happened
since the beginning of time.

163
00:09:25,840 --> 00:09:29,560
A young woman did not fart
in her husband's embrace.

164
00:09:29,580 --> 00:09:33,448
Four thousand years later, the
fart joke is still undefeated.

165
00:09:33,468 --> 00:09:37,810
A Greek joke book called the Philogelos,
the Laughter Lover, has more than two

166
00:09:37,830 --> 00:09:39,723
hundred and sixty jokes.

167
00:09:39,743 --> 00:09:44,148
In one, a chatty barber asks a
man, how should I cut your hair?

168
00:09:44,168 --> 00:09:46,802
And the man says, in silence.

169
00:09:46,822 --> 00:09:52,351
That joke was already being told about
a king of Macedon, centuries earlier.

170
00:09:52,371 --> 00:09:56,751
Many of the book's jokes are about
a well-meaning fool, an egghead.

171
00:09:56,771 --> 00:10:01,314
One egghead dreamed he stepped on a
nail, so in the morning, he bandaged his

172
00:10:01,334 --> 00:10:04,714
foot. His friend heard
why, and said, no wonder.

173
00:10:04,734 --> 00:10:07,262
Why do you sleep without shoes on?

174
00:10:07,282 --> 00:10:09,462
So were ancient humans ever bored?

175
00:10:09,482 --> 00:10:11,429
Of course they were, sometimes.

176
00:10:11,449 --> 00:10:13,432
But here's something strange.

177
00:10:13,452 --> 00:10:16,619
The English word boredom
is surprisingly new.

178
00:10:16,639 --> 00:10:21,144
It only shows up in print in the eighteen
twenties, and Charles Dickens helped

179
00:10:21,164 --> 00:10:23,897
make it popular in the eighteen fifties.

180
00:10:23,917 --> 00:10:27,297
Today, many of us find doing
nothing surprisingly hard.

181
00:10:27,317 --> 00:10:32,660
In two thousand fourteen, psychologists
left people alone in a room for fifteen

182
00:10:32,680 --> 00:10:35,410
minutes, with nothing to do but think.

183
00:10:35,430 --> 00:10:40,110
Many of them chose to give themselves a
small electric shock, rather than just

184
00:10:40,130 --> 00:10:43,931
sit there. Our ancestors
rarely had that problem.

185
00:10:43,951 --> 00:10:46,251
They were almost never alone.

186
00:10:46,271 --> 00:10:48,426
And look at everything we just saw.

187
00:10:48,446 --> 00:10:52,013
Stories, games, toys, sport, music, jokes.

188
00:10:52,033 --> 00:10:54,496
Almost none of it was done alone.

189
00:10:54,516 --> 00:10:57,871
Before we go, which ancient
fun would you try first?

190
00:10:57,891 --> 00:11:03,084
Firelight stories, the Royal Game of
Ur, or the four thousand year old joke?

191
00:11:03,104 --> 00:11:05,060
Let me know in the comments.

192
00:11:05,080 --> 00:11:07,635
Tonight, you'll probably
pick up your phone.

193
00:11:07,655 --> 00:11:12,355
For three hundred thousand years, they
picked up a story, a game, a song.

194
00:11:12,375 --> 00:11:15,089
You scroll alone. They laughed together.
